{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"=== HANDOFF #21 — e369. READ THIS, THEN #20, THEN #19 + ITS ADDENDUM. ===\n\nWORKTREE /Users/joeoakhart/rebar-jira-family (branch worktree-main, ff'd to gerrit/main).\nALWAYS: export PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\". CLOSES from /Users/joeoakhart/agent-5200-diag (branch\ncloses4), already ff'd to 6baa030460 — `--ref` does NOT satisfy the file_impact precheck.\nDO NOT trust this board; read the tickets.\n\n*** THE HEADLINE: 2b16's RE-CERTIFIED PLAN IS ALSO WRONG, AND IMPLEMENTING IT WOULD SHIP DATA LOSS. ***\n*** I did not start the implementation. This is the second specification failure on this ticket, and ***\n*** the SAME class as the first: an untraced claim about what `managed_refs` means. ***\n\nThe amended rule is \"ref IS managed + peer lacks it -> the peer DELETED it -> inbound REMOVE wins, and\nsuppress the outbound ADD\". Its safety argument is \"an UNMANAGED dep is never removed, so a brand-new\nlocal dep is safe by construction\". **A brand-new local dep is NOT unmanaged.** `add_managed_ref` is\nfolded by the LINK-event processor (`reducer/_processors.py:411`, inside `process_link`), so a ref is\nmanaged the instant it is created LOCALLY. The plan states both facts and they contradict each other.\n\nMEASURED against gerrit/main @ b6916709f3 (probe, not reasoning):\n  after a LOCAL link event, managed_refs = [['blocks','local-b']]; should_propagate_removal -> True\n  (a) `_diff_link_removals_inbound`, brand-new never-pushed dep, issuelinks observed [] -> EMITS the removal\n  (b) `compute_inbound_mutations`, no outbound ctx                                        -> EMITS the removal\n  (c) `compute_inbound_mutations` WITH the outbound link ADD a real pass emits              -> []\nRead (c) against (b): **the landed G4 outbound-add suppression is the ONLY thing standing between a\nbrand-new local link and its deletion**, and deleting that suppression is the amended plan's central\ninstruction. Net effect if implemented: a human creates a link, the next pass emits an inbound REMOVE\n(managed + peer lacks it), the arbitration suppresses the outbound ADD, and the link is DESTROYED\ninstead of pushed. Strictly worse than today's no-op.\n\n**WHY NO PATCH TO THE RULE CAN FIX IT.** \"brand-new local link never pushed\" and \"link we pushed that\nthe peer deleted\" are STATE-IDENTICAL: managed=True, target bound=True, issuelinks observed=True, peer\nlink absent=True. Every input the guards read is the same. So ANY arbitration that is a function of\n(managed, bound, observed, current peer set) MUST answer both the same way, and one answer is data\nloss. It is a MISSING-SIGNAL problem, not a tuning problem. The discriminator can only be HISTORY.\nCorollary: the existing green test `test_peer_deleted_link_emits_a_removal` is fed the brand-new-link\nstate — it is named for one scenario and pins both.\n\n**THE HISTORY SIGNAL ALREADY EXISTS** (traced; this makes \"build it right\" much cheaper than the plan's\nrejected alternative). ADR 0026's per-binding BASELINE — `binding_store.set_baseline`\n(`binding_store.py:453-467`), advanced EVERY PASS by `reconcile._advance_baselines`\n(`reconcile.py:519-536`), persisted by the existing binding-store commit path, \"no new commit surface\".\nIts absence semantics are already the fail-safe we need and already documented: \"An ABSENT baseline is\nVALID and degrades to local-wins; a version-1 store reads fine.\" Arbitration becomes: in the baseline's\npeer link set AND absent from the current OBSERVED set -> peer deleted it (inbound wins, suppress the\noutbound ADD); not in the baseline -> never pushed (outbound ADD wins); no baseline -> local-wins.\n`managed_refs` drops OUT of the arbitration, which is correct — it cannot answer the question.\nImplementation notes on the ticket: own key on the binding entry (do NOT widen `_BASELINE_FIELDS`, the\n5-scalar three-way-merge vocabulary), store in LOCAL relation vocabulary via `link_direction`, and\n**advance the recorded set only when the pass OBSERVED issuelinks** or a fail-open read poisons the\nhistory into \"the peer deleted everything\". Also: `binding_store` is ALREADY a parameter of both link\ndiffers while `prev_snapshot` is not, so this needs NO new differ parameter.\n\n**SCOPING FACT THAT DECIDES WHETHER 2b16 GATES e369 — VERIFIED.** It is a SHARED-CORE gap, identical on\nCloud. `compute_inbound_mutations` has ONE call site (`run_differs.py:641`, backend-agnostic) and both\nbackends implement the same `map_remote_links` port (`adapters/jira/backend.py:219`,\n`adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py:397`). So Cloud does not mirror peer link deletions either and never\nhas. Only DC noticed because only DC has the cell: Cloud's three live link cells\n(`test_link_primitives_roundtrip_live`, `test_link_sync_differ_apply_roundtrip_live`,\n`test_link_sync_writes_absolute_direction_live`) never delete a link on the peer. Today's behaviour on\nBOTH backends is local-wins-and-resurrect: the peer-side deletion is reverted next pass, no local data\ndestroyed — it FAILS SAFE, the same shape as the `e39f` ruling.\n=> MY RECOMMENDATION TO THE OPERATOR: **DEFER 2b16** (document local-wins, xfail the live cell WITH the\nreason — do not invert it, file the baseline-history arbitration as a follow-up). Building it in a hurry\nis the higher-risk path for \"ship defect-free\", because a wrong rule deletes real links on both backends.\nA parity follow-up also falls out for 3e73: Cloud is missing the inbound-link-deletion cell, so a green\nCloud suite currently implies coverage that does not exist. **AWAITING THE OPERATOR'S CALL.**\n\n--- WHAT LANDED / WHAT IS IN FLIGHT ---\n * **change 1288 (88d9) SUBMITTED AND MERGED as 6baa030460.** Its CI went fully green on patchset 3; the\n   earlier `-1`s were the stale-base/cancellation traps, as #20 predicted. LOC on main now:\n   inbound_differ 773, inbound_fields 351, fetcher 707, outbound_links 178, apply_inbound_records 667 —\n   all under the 800 cap, and 88d9's net-negative requirement on inbound_differ (797 -> 773) is met.\n * **HARNESS RUN 30828166841 dispatched on main @ 6baa030460** (job \"jira DC harness smoke\", started\n   15:37:46Z, ~24 min). NOTE A TRAP I HIT: my first dispatch (30828109189) landed on 216452d8ca — the\n   commit BEFORE 1288's merge — because the GitHub mirror had not yet received it. **After a Gerrit\n   submit, `git fetch origin main` and confirm the mirror has your commit BEFORE dispatching a workflow\n   on `main`, or the run silently excludes what you just landed.** Cancelled and re-dispatched.\n * 88d9 is otherwise READY TO CLOSE (`--class preexisting`): mutation ledger, consumer sweep (both\n   `\"parent\" in fields` and `fields[\"parent\"]` forms), integration suites, lint/typecheck and the LOC\n   caps are all recorded on the ticket. It waits ONLY on the live cell's verdict, because its AC accepts\n   either GREEN or the explicit `SETUP FAILED` platform branch (DC refuses to null a sub-task's\n   `fields.parent`). Record the run id + that verdict, then close.\n * 5200 waits ONLY on this run's verdicts; its 18 ACs are already independently verified (comment 31 +\n   the merges in comment 35). Its own comment enumerates which cells are EXPECTED RED — do not invert them.\n * e369: 11 of 13 ACs MET, AC1 blocked only on the children, AC5 already AMENDED. **Its plan review is\n   `stale-code` (confirmed this session: 22 dependency files drifted) and must be re-run IMMEDIATELY\n   before the close** — main moves constantly, so run it last, not early.\n\n--- PROCESS NOTES FROM THIS STRETCH ---\n 1. The mirror-lag trap above. It would have produced a \"fresh\" harness run that proved nothing about\n    the change just merged, and nothing in the run output would have said so.\n 2. Verifying ONE load-bearing premise of a certified plan cost ~30 lines of probe and saved shipping a\n    data-loss bug. `review-plan` certified the plan because it is internally consistent about a false\n    premise — **the gate reads the plan, not the reducer.** When a plan's safety rests on a claim about\n    what a projection MEANS, drive the projection and look.\n 3. `rebar comment` bodies were written to a file and passed as `\"$(cat file)\"`, then GREPPED back for\n    expected identifiers. All four comments verified intact. Keep doing exactly that."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785771731627942001,"uuid":"22d7ace1-f642-4278-9415-2aa02c830709"}